OBDLink SX timing boundary probe — 2026-10-02

Hardware: OBDLink SX, USB VCP COM4 at 115200, Ford connected, ignition on,
engine off. OBDWiz and FORScan were closed. Device identity: STN1130 v5.10.1.
Protocol: ISO 15765-4 CAN 11/500. This used only generic Mode 01 RPM reads;
no ECU writes, DTC operations, OEM requests, or NVM commands were sent.

Commands sent, in order:
  STI
  ATE0
  STPRS
  STPO
  AT H0
  AT SH 7DF
  STPTO 102
  ATAT 1
  30 x STPX D:010C, R:1, T:34
  30 x STPX D:010C, R:1, T:33
  ATAT 2
  30 x STPX D:010C, R:1, T:34
  30 x STPX D:010C, R:1, T:33

Results from the COM4 serial polling loop:
  ATAT1, T=34: 30/30 valid, 30.04 valid responses/s, 0.999 s batch
  ATAT1, T=33: 30/30 valid, 33.58 valid responses/s, 0.893 s batch
  ATAT2, T=34: 30/30 valid, 32.56 valid responses/s, 0.921 s batch
  ATAT2, T=33: 30/30 valid, 32.86 valid responses/s, 0.913 s batch

The final cleanup commands and replies were:
  STPPMC -> OK
  STPTO 102 -> OK
  ATAT 1 -> OK
  AT H0 -> OK
  AT SH 7DF -> OK
  ATE1 -> OK
  STPX D:010C, R:1, T:250 -> 41 0C 09 70 (valid RPM response)

The T=33 single-run sample did not show a loss, but its rate is consistent with
the earlier ~33.3/s STPX plateau and is not a repeatable improvement claim.
Earlier T=30 tests lost 4–6 of 20 responses. No baud or ATAT setting has
produced the user's reported >=50 PID/s OBDWiz rate.
